python3-paramiko-legacy
=======================

This is paramiko with legacy, insecure SSH algorithms restored. It exists to
talk to old devices whose SSH servers cannot be upgraded. Do not use it for
anything else.

It installs the module "paramiko_legacy", never "paramiko", so it sits beside
python3-paramiko without changing it. Code opts in explicitly:

    import paramiko_legacy as paramiko

    client = paramiko.SSHClient()
    client.set_missing_host_key_policy(paramiko.RejectPolicy())
    client.load_host_keys("/path/to/known_hosts")
    client.connect("old-switch.example", username="admin", password="...")

Restored, relative to paramiko 5:

  * ssh-dss (DSA) host and user keys, including DSSKey;
  * ssh-rsa (RSA with SHA-1) signatures;
  * diffie-hellman-group1-sha1, diffie-hellman-group14-sha1 and
    diffie-hellman-group-exchange-sha1 key exchange;
  * group-exchange moduli down to 1024 bits (5.0 requires 2048);
  * GSSAPI authentication and key exchange (needs python3-gssapi).

CBC ciphers (aes*-cbc, 3des-cbc) and hmac-sha1/hmac-md5 were never removed
upstream and are available in both packages.

Modern algorithms are still preferred: a legacy one is only negotiated when the
peer offers nothing better. To pin a session to exactly what an old device
needs, use Transport.get_security_options() or the disabled_algorithms
argument to SSHClient.connect().

RSA user keys and servers that advertise more than they accept: OpenSSH 9.2
(Debian bookworm) sends rsa-sha2-256/512 in server-sig-algs even when its
PubkeyAcceptedAlgorithms only allows ssh-rsa (OpenSSH 10.0 does not), and
paramiko believes the advertisement. If RSA authentication is rejected by
such a server, force SHA-1 signatures:

    client.connect(..., disabled_algorithms={
        "pubkeys": ["rsa-sha2-512", "rsa-sha2-256"]})

Servers too old to know rsa-sha2 do not send server-sig-algs at all, and then
ssh-rsa is chosen automatically.

Loggers are named "paramiko_legacy.*", so their output can be told apart from
the stock module's.

Source and apt repository: https://github.com/mithro/paramiko-legacy
